<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>It says what it is doing, even off.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    What the device says before it has a position, when it is off, and when its battery runs out.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">On the apron, and in the bag</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="waiting">It says <span class="highlight">what it is waiting for</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        One press turns it on and opens the traffic display. Until it has a
        position, the ring says so. The word below it tells you how far
        the receiver has got: silent, sending position solutions with no date
        yet, or reading a date from a satellite.
      </p>
      <p>
        That tells you whether to wait or carry the device into the open.
        It announces its first position, and the display
        starts showing the sky around you.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "no-fix.png", alt: "The radar page with no position: the empty ring shows NO FIX and, below it, the receiver's progress" %>
      <figcaption>Waiting for the sky</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="off">The logo on the screen <span class="highlight">means off</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Hold the button for two seconds to switch it off. The radio stops, the screen
        gets three seconds to draw the logo, and power is cut when you let
        go. E-paper keeps its image with no power at all, so the logo stays
        on the screen while the device is off.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the same hold with a finger resting on the pad to leave the screen blank
        instead. An image left on e-paper for a winter leaves a permanent shadow.
        Leave the screen blank when you put the device
        away for storage.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "off.png", alt: "The screen of a device that has been switched off: the skyBlip logo, kept on the e-paper screen with no power" %>
      <figcaption>Off</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="flat-battery">When the battery runs out, <span class="highlight">the screen says so</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        At 3.2&nbsp;V the device switches itself off. Its last message tells you why.
        You pressed nothing, so only the screen can explain the shutdown.
        Without that message, it would look as though the device had crashed or
        failed. The button stops working at the same moment, because a press
        would only bring the battery back to the same cutoff voltage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Plug in the cable to use the device again. That single action
        wakes the electronics without starting the device and puts the logo back:
        the device is off again and charging. The next press starts
        it. If the battery ran out on a shelf, the device left no message.
        In that case, pressing the button displays the same flat-battery message.
      </p>
      <p>
        The device remembers this the next time it starts. Its self test reads
        <span class="mono">WAS EMPTY</span> beside the voltage. A phone that
        connects during that startup is told the same, so a device
        that went dark does not come back as if nothing happened.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "off-flat-battery.png", alt: "The screen of a device that switched itself off on an empty battery: the skyBlip logo with the words BATTERY EMPTY under it" %>
      <figcaption>Out of battery</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>From off</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Press the button</th><td>starts, unless the battery is under 3.4&nbsp;V with no charger connected. In that case, the screen says <span class="mono">BATTERY EMPTY</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press after a flat-battery shutdown</th><td>does nothing. The screen already says why it stopped. Connect the cable to use the device again</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug the charger in</th><td>charges and stays off. Connecting a cable in a flight bag does not tell it to transmit</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug the charger into a device with a flat battery</th><td>charges and stays off. The logo replaces <span class="mono">BATTERY EMPTY</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hold the button and plug the charger in</th><td>starts and charges</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press <span class="mono">RST</span></th><td>restarts. Press twice inside half a second to start the USB bootloader, which lets you install firmware over USB</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>

  <h3 id="cell">Why the battery lasts the day.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Switching off takes a sequence of steps, not a sudden power cut. Their order
    matters. The external flash memory is told to sleep while it still has
    power to receive the command. After the power cut, that command does
    nothing. The memory would then draw its full standby current all night
    while the device sits in a bag looking switched off.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The radio and sensors account for the rest of the power use. In the air, the device transmits once a
    second. On the ground, it transmits once every ten seconds. Its address determines which of the ten
    seconds it uses, so aircraft on the same airfield spread their transmissions out.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Radio asleep before its reset line</li>
    <li>Shutdown follows this order: flash memory, receiver, power supplies, pins</li>
    <li>The device transmits at 1&nbsp;Hz in the air, 0.1&nbsp;Hz on the ground</li>
    <li>The sensor hub&rsquo;s gyroscope is never read: it draws a milliamp</li>
    <li>Charging is refused outside 5 to 60&nbsp;&deg;C</li>
    <li>The light blinks and never stays on continuously</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="gauge">What the gauge will not do.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    It uses two voltage curves, not one. During charging, the terminal voltage is above the
    battery&rsquo;s own voltage. The same reading then means a much emptier battery,
    and the percentage says which curve it read.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It never walks backwards, and a 22&nbsp;dBm transmission that briefly lowers the voltage does not
    move it. A pilot stops trusting a gauge that jumps
    whenever the radio transmits.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Threshold</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>3.60&nbsp;V</th><td>the battery is low, about 12%: the light blinks red, the radar says BAT and the percentage, and switching off leaves CHARGE THE BATTERY on the screen</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.50&nbsp;V</th><td>the battery is critical, about 5%: the light blinks every 600&nbsp;ms. From here, the device saves nothing to permanent memory except the flight record, which provides evidence of the flight that is ending</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.40&nbsp;V</th><td>the device refuses to start on a battery this flat. The button stays disabled so pressure inside a bag cannot trigger repeated attempts</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.20&nbsp;V</th><td>the battery is flat: the device switches itself off and leaves BATTERY EMPTY on the screen. Each threshold takes three consecutive readings to enter and three to leave. A brief voltage drop during transmission cannot trigger a change, and a battery near a threshold cannot keep switching between states</td></tr>
        <tr><th>1.80&nbsp;V</th><td>below this, the reading comes from a floating voltage divider, not a flat battery. The device does not act on it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

